WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.750
Consolidated picking with island
sorting, supported by AMR robots.

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.390
Simulation of a full day of operation.

3
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:11.820
We are building a simulation of
consolidated picking with island sorting,

4
00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:13.080
supported by AMR robots,

5
00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:17.170
to generate savings compared with running the
same process with store-by-store

6
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:17.590
picking.

7
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.680
With store-by-store picking,

8
00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.700
a picker walks the warehouse
building each store's order,

9
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:26.790
and the same item is searched for again and
again. With consolidated picking,

10
00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:30.610
each item is taken from the rack only once,
for all the stores together,

11
00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.840
and then it is distributed at the islands.

12
00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:38.360
The case: 105 stores, 15 trucks of 7
stores each, 4 rolls per store,

13
00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:42.710
almost a thousand items and more than
nine thousand cartons.

14
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.170
Three sorting islands and 10 AMRs.

15
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.420
There are two kinds of picking. Pallet
picking: full single-item pallets,

16
00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:54.560
brought down by the reach truck. And
consolidated carton picking:

17
00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:56.410
a picker builds consolidated,

18
00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.680
multi-item pallets with the cartons that
do not make a full pallet.

19
00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:05.330
A pallet that reaches the island by AMR can
be single-item or multi-item.

20
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:10.410
This is how the day looks. The reach truck
brings full pallets down from the racks,

21
00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.570
the picker builds the consolidated ones,

22
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:16.460
and the AMRs take them to the transfer
area, the buffer and the islands.

23
00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:17.890
At the island,

24
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.660
the operator takes each item from the pallet
and distributes it into the rolls of

25
00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.260
the stores that ordered it.

26
00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:29.030
A light shows which roll each carton goes into.
A roll is a caged cart on wheels,

27
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:30.510
and each store gets four.

28
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.290
When a roll is full, a roll handler removes it
and leaves an empty one in its place.

29
00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:39.180
The full roll goes through the picking check,
which inspects 15% of the rolls,

30
00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:43.220
and only then is it wrapped and left in the
staging area of the dock of the truck

31
00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:44.170
that will carry it.

32
00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.910
And the empty rolls: they do not
appear out of nowhere.

33
00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:52.480
Today the delivery trucks bring back the rolls
they took yesterday. They arrive,

34
00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.370
unload seven stacks of four empty rolls,
that is 28 rolls per truck,

35
00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:00.710
the same ones they take today, and load the
full rolls built the day before.

36
00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.480
The empty-roll team cleans each stack,

37
00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:07.730
assembles the rolls and takes them to the
empty stock next to the islands.

38
00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.310
The operators take them from there. If the
stock ran out, the island would wait:

39
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.320
there is no magic with empty rolls.

40
00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:18.620
That is why the day starts with the staging
area full of yesterday's loads,

41
00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:20.790
which is the most demanding condition.

42
00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.320
With all this, the simulation checks six
things: that staging does not saturate,

43
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:29.000
that the buffer does not saturate, that the
islands have work all the time,

44
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.710
that every truck is loaded on time,

45
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.210
that every truck uses the dock
closest to its load,

46
00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.860
and how many people and how much
equipment are needed.

47
00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:41.650
Staging peaked at 464 occupied positions
and never ran out of space.

48
00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:47.900
The buffer has 122 places and reached 87. The
transfer area has 52 and reached 51:

49
00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:51.100
it is at the limit. If any area filled up,

50
00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:54.990
the simulation stops and reports
that it collapsed.

51
00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.650
The islands work almost non-stop from three in
the afternoon until four thirty in

52
00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:01.400
the morning,

53
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:04.900
at around 88% utilization. They
only drop at the 10 p.m.

54
00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:08.150
shift change and during a short
break at two thirty.

55
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.360
The best dock: each truck goes to the
free dock closest to its load,

56
00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:16.610
and the loads are spread over time so that each
truck gets the dock that is near

57
00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:17.460
what it carries.

58
00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:20.970
The 15 morning trucks load the
rolls built yesterday,

59
00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.750
and so do the 15 of the following day,
and all of them leave on time:

60
00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.480
the longest delay was 2.6 minutes, within
the five-minute tolerance.

61
00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.760
With three eight-hour shifts,

62
00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.350
seven net hours per person and 15%
allowances built into the times,

63
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.150
the simulation gives the minimum staffing:
eight people in the morning,

64
00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.730
ten in the afternoon and fifteen at
night. Staff are multi-skilled,

65
00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.140
and include the receiving and
picking checks,

66
00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:48.370
and the administrative staff who release the
orders and give the exit approval.
